Welcome to OSPF lesson 101
Goal:
1. Add hostnames: 
	R1 to R1
	R2 to R2 
	R3 to R3
2. Add loopback interfaces such that:
	R1 is 1.1.1.1 /32 
	R2 is 2.2.2.2 /32
	R3 is 3.3.3.3 /32
3. Set Router-ids
	R1 to 1.1.1.1
	etc.
	NOTE that if the router already has active Neighbors, the new Router ID will NOT take effect. It will need to have the OSPF process cleared, which shuts down the network and must restart it. This is very bad on live production networks. 
4. Configure all Routers to be OSPF neighbors and advertising their routes.
5. Configure LAN-facing interfaces passive. 
6. Adjust OSPF interface cost to favor the path though R1.


- Experiment: Change the hello timer on one router and observe where adjacency breaks.

Topology:
Device	| IP address	| Subnet	| 
R1 	| G0/0/0 = 10.1.2.1 	| 255.255.255.0; 
R1 	| G0/0/1 = 10.1.4.2 	| 255.255.255.0
R1 	| G0/0/2 = 192.168.1.254	| 255.255.255.0

R2 	| G0/0/0 = 10.1.2.2 /24; 
R2 	| G0/0/1 = 10.1.3.1 /24; 
R2 	| G0/0/2 = 192.168.2.254 /24; 

R3 	| G0/0/0 = 10.1.3.2 	| 255.255.255.0; 
R3 	| G0/0/1 = 10.1.4.1 	| 255.255.255.0; 
R3 	| G0/0/2 = 192.168.3.254 	| 255.255.255.0; 

PC1 	| F0/0 192.168.1.1 	| 255.255.255.0; 	| DG = Not Set
PC2 	| F0/0 192.168.2.1 	| 255.255.255.0; 	| DG = Not Set
PC3 	| F0/0 192.168.3.1 	| 255.255.255.0; 	| DG = Not Set

Steps to complete
1. Use hostname command to change hostname of device
  - Router#(config) hostname R1
2. Use interface command to implicity create the loopback interface. Give them addresses based on the number of the router. 
  - R1#(config)interface Loopback1  
  - R1#(config-int) ip address 1.1.1.1 255.255.255.255
3. Set router-ids according to their loopback interfaces
  - R1#(config-router) router-id 1.1.1.1
4. Enable OSPF process 1 on all router interface facing other routes(modern per-interface method).
  - R1#(config) interface GigabitEthernet0/0/0
  - R1#(config-int) ip ospf 1
5. Set passive-interface on router interfacing facing LANs. 
  - R1#(config) router ospf 1
  - R1#config-router) passive-interface GigabitEthernet0/0/1

6. Adjust link cost so that traffic preffers to be routed though R1.
  Either 
    Raise R2 and R3 so that they are more expenseive.
    - R2#(config) int G0/0/0
    - R2#config-router) ip ospf cost 3000
    - R3#(config) int G0/0/1
    - R3#config-router) ip ospf cost 3000
  OR
    Reduce R1's links to R2 and R3 so that is cheaper. 
    - R1#(config) int G0/0/0
    - R1#config-router) ip ospf cost 1
    - R1#(config) int G0/0/1
    - R1#config-router) ip ospf cost 1

Verify using show commands to verify neighbor configuration.
  Show ip ospf 1
  show ip ospf neighbors
  show ip ospf database 
Verify setup works by pinging or traceroute : PC1 to PC2, PC2 to PC3 and PC3 to PC1. 

What I learned
that traceroute is a helpful tool. 
that topology maps entries require Designated Gateways if it is a PC/end device. 
Passive-interface command prevents the transmission and reception of OSPF hello messages will still advertise the network. 

